The go_qnx_exec wrapper runs qnx/arm binaries on a remote QNX device
over ssh. QNX ARM targets are usually devices that don't host the Go
toolchain, so, like Android and iOS, their tests run on the device while
the toolchain runs on a host.

When the Go toolchain is built for qnx/arm, cmd/dist compiles this
program into $GOROOT/bin/go_qnx_arm_exec, which the go command uses as
the default -exec wrapper for qnx/arm (see go help run and go help test).

The device is described entirely by environment variables, so nothing
about a particular machine is stored here:

	GOQNX_EXEC_SSH      ssh destination (required), for example a host
	                    defined in the user's ssh config.
	GOQNX_EXEC_RUN      directory the binary is copied to and executed
	                    from; it must permit execution. Default /tmp.
	GOQNX_EXEC_TMPDIR   a directory on a real filesystem that the run
	                    user can write (optional). When set, each run
	                    gets a subdirectory holding a copy of the test's
	                    package directory (testdata included) as the
	                    working directory, with the test's TMPDIR beside
	                    it. Without it, tests that need a real directory
	                    or their testdata fail.
	GOQNX_EXEC_USER     run the binary as this user with "on -u"
	                    (optional); some QNX configurations refuse to
	                    execute an untrusted file as root.
	GOQNX_EXEC_STAGE    a directory to copy the binary to before the run
	                    directory (optional), for devices whose run
	                    directory cannot be written to directly.

Only GOQNX_EXEC_SSH is required. The others default or are omitted, so a
QNX board without the execution and filesystem restrictions of a locked
device needs only the ssh destination.

A single ssh connection is reused (ssh ControlMaster), so a package's
tests are one login. The wrapper will not remove a device directory that
holds a Unix socket name, since unlinking one can wedge QNX's network
stack; it leaves and reports it instead.

For example, to run the standard library tests for qnx/arm:

	export GOQNX_EXEC_SSH=mydevice
	GOOS=qnx GOARCH=arm GOARM=7 go test runtime
